1
00:00:00,059 --> 00:00:03,400
Call notes keep an incident update with
the call it belongs to.

2
00:00:03,849 --> 00:00:07,260
Begin by reading the title and call number
in Call Details.

3
00:00:07,679 --> 00:00:13,510
This example is Training Only, Responder
call and check-in practice, F D fifty-one,

4
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:16,329
twenty twenty-six, zero zero zero one.

5
00:00:16,790 --> 00:00:20,479
Its Nature describes a fictional
industrial alarm exercise.

6
00:00:20,909 --> 00:00:24,920
Check the incident identity before adding
information, especially when

7
00:00:24,920 --> 00:00:26,829
several calls have similar names.

8
00:00:27,360 --> 00:00:29,719
Choose Notes to open this call's notes.

9
00:00:29,890 --> 00:00:34,539
The Call Notes panel has a search field,
an entry area and Add Note.

10
00:00:35,039 --> 00:00:39,129
In this prepared example, the entry
records that a virtual access plan

11
00:00:39,129 --> 00:00:43,180
was reviewed in the Academy software
exercise, with no real response

12
00:00:43,239 --> 00:00:45,050
or physical inspection requested.

13
00:00:45,499 --> 00:00:50,690
Use a clear update that distinguishes an
observation, a request and completed work.

14
00:00:51,120 --> 00:00:53,549
Review the text before choosing Add Note.

15
00:00:54,010 --> 00:00:57,010
Avoid putting an update on the wrong
incident simply because

16
00:00:57,010 --> 00:00:58,739
a notes panel is already open.

17
00:00:59,010 --> 00:01:01,729
Reopen Call Notes and read the saved
entry.

18
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:06,139
The same complete text now appears in the
list with Wallace Boden as

19
00:01:06,139 --> 00:01:08,270
its author and a recorded timestamp.

20
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:10,859
The entry area below is empty again.

21
00:01:11,430 --> 00:01:16,470
Verify the actual saved text as well as
its author; a prepared message in an entry

22
00:01:16,470 --> 00:01:18,580
field is different from a listed note.

23
00:01:18,929 --> 00:01:21,800
This gives the next person reviewing the
call a specific

24
00:01:21,830 --> 00:01:23,859
update they can find and understand.

25
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:26,430
On the same call, open the Check-In tab.

26
00:01:26,890 --> 00:01:31,500
This view shows Wallace Boden, the
Personnel type, a timer and Check In.

27
00:01:31,969 --> 00:01:35,239
The recorded timer has forty-two minutes
and twenty-eight seconds

28
00:01:35,239 --> 00:01:38,989
remaining, with seventeen point five of
sixty minutes elapsed.

29
00:01:39,390 --> 00:01:42,239
Read whose timer you are viewing before
taking action.

30
00:01:42,719 --> 00:01:45,549
Quick Check-In and Check-In History are
also visible.

31
00:01:45,979 --> 00:01:49,750
For this exercise, choose the Check In
button on Wallace Boden's card

32
00:01:49,750 --> 00:01:51,500
to review the named confirmation.

33
00:01:51,839 --> 00:01:55,569
The sheet identifies Select Check-In Type,
Wallace Boden.

34
00:01:55,990 --> 00:01:59,049
Personnel is selected, and Add Note is
optional.

35
00:01:59,520 --> 00:02:03,499
This exercise note explicitly describes a
virtual personnel check-in

36
00:02:03,499 --> 00:02:05,220
for Academy software practice.

37
00:02:05,619 --> 00:02:08,909
A software entry should accurately
describe what was recorded.

38
00:02:09,389 --> 00:02:14,860
Check the named person, selected type and
note, then choose Confirm Check-In once.

39
00:02:15,290 --> 00:02:19,569
Keep actual personnel accountability and
any missed check-in response aligned

40
00:02:19,569 --> 00:02:21,130
with your department's procedures.

41
00:02:21,319 --> 00:02:25,039
The returned Personnel card now shows
fifty-nine minutes and thirty-eight

42
00:02:25,039 --> 00:02:29,300
seconds remaining and Last check-in, zero
point one minutes ago.

43
00:02:29,830 --> 00:02:34,179
Below it, the expanded Check-In History
contains a Personnel entry, the same

44
00:02:34,179 --> 00:02:36,050
practice note and its timestamp.

45
00:02:36,490 --> 00:02:39,370
Review the saved history as well as the
changed timer.

46
00:02:39,909 --> 00:02:44,259
This recording demonstrates the software
check-in; it does not establish physical

47
00:02:44,259 --> 00:02:46,339
arrival or a field safety check.

48
00:02:46,710 --> 00:02:50,540
A visible saved entry is the result to
verify before relying on the

49
00:02:50,540 --> 00:02:52,200
software record in a handover.

50
00:02:52,459 --> 00:02:56,229
Community paramedicine and mental health
response teams can use clear

51
00:02:56,259 --> 00:02:58,280
updates during coordinated outreach.

52
00:02:58,749 --> 00:03:02,849
Search-and-rescue teams can keep support
staff informed, security teams

53
00:03:02,849 --> 00:03:06,920
can document post updates, wildland
contractors can coordinate crew

54
00:03:06,920 --> 00:03:11,570
activity, and industrial emergency teams
can maintain standby accountability.

55
00:03:12,139 --> 00:03:15,219
Fire and EMS departments can apply the
same habits.

56
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:19,889
Select the intended call, record only what
you mean, and inspect the

57
00:03:19,889 --> 00:03:23,889
saved note or check-in history before
handing over to another team member.

